<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omar al-Mukhtar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lion of the Desert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Omar al-Mukhtar was a Libyan teacher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Senussi leader who led armed resistance to Italian colonial rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> for nearly twenty years. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the Jebel Akhdar ended when Italian forces captured him in 1931, and he was hanged at Suluq that September. He is honoured as a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and across the Arab world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omar-al-mukhtar.jpg" alt="Omar al-Mukhtar" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Italian colonial administration of Libya [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Omar_Mukhtar_13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is marked by the internment of much of Cyrenaica's population in camps and the execution of the resistance leader <b>Omar Mukhtar</b> in 1931, and by the massacre in Addis Ababa that followed the attempt on Viceroy Rodolfo Graziani's life in February 1937.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Cyrenaica, Rodolfo Graziani deported much of the nomadic population into concentration camps where tens of thousands died, and <b>Omar al-Mukhtar</b> was hanged in 1931.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-libya">Italian Libya</a></li><li><blockquote>The Senussi fought France in Chad from 1902 and Italian Libya from 1911; <b>Omar al-Mukhtar</b> led resistance until his execution in 1931.</blockquote><a href="/state/senussi-order">Senussi Order</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.28,21.63&amp;year=1905" aria-label="Cyrenaica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5106 990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Omar al-Mukhtar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.28,21.63&amp;year=1905">What was here in 1905? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
